<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Ockeghem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1410–<a href="/years/1497">1497</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Okeghem</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Ockeghem was a Franco-Flemish composer and singer of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, the leading European composer between Guillaume Du Fay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a>. Probably born in Saint-Ghislain in the County of Hainaut, he is first recorded as a singer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>'s cathedral in 1443, and from around 1452 spent most of his career at the French royal court under Charles VII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> and Charles VIII. Some 14 masses, five motets and 21 chansons are reliably attributed to him, among them the canon-based Missa prolationum and the earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem. His death in 1497 was lamented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, Jean Molinet and Josquin, who wrote Nymphes des bois for him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-ockeghem.jpg" alt="Johannes Ockeghem" width="330" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Portrait_of_a_Musician,_64.54.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem, by <b>Johannes Ockeghem</b>, does not include it; the first settings to include it are by Engarandus Juvenis (1490) and Antoine Brumel (1516), and later ones by Charpentier, Delalande, Mozart, Berlioz, Verdi, Britten and Stravinsky.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Taruskin argued that Busnois wrote the earliest, while other scholars favour Du Fay; Guillaume Faugues, Regis and <b>Johannes Ockeghem</b> may also have written settings in the 1450s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.39,0.69&amp;year=1466" aria-label="Tours on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4566 502 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Johannes Ockeghem"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5016 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5016 772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.39,0.69&amp;year=1466">What was here in 1466? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
